   <p class="electric-byline">LeadsFlo Editorial · November 4, 2026</p>
   <div class="electric-summary">
    <strong>THE SHORT ANSWER</strong>
    <p>Cost per lead is a starting metric. Electrical contractors should evaluate qualified inquiries, confirmed estimates, completed jobs and the contribution margins behind those outcomes.</p>
   </div>
   <div class="electric-cta">
    <strong>ELECTRICIAN LEAD GENERATION</strong>
    <p>Explore our approach to electrical advertising, qualification, CRM follow-up and booked estimates.</p>
    <a href="/electrician-lead-generation/">Explore Electrician Lead Generation ↗</a>
   </div>
   <section class='lf-article-section'><h2>Understand what cost per lead measures</h2><p>Cost per lead is campaign spending divided by attributed inquiries. It does not tell you whether those inquiries are reachable, inside the service area or suitable for the electrical work your company performs.</p><p>Differentiate website visits, phone clicks, answered calls, submitted forms and confirmed estimates. Those events represent different customer milestones.</p></section>
<section class='lf-article-section'><h2>Calculate qualified lead costs</h2><p>Define qualification rules first: valid contact information, a service the contractor performs, geographic coverage and a realistic customer request.</p><p>For illustration only, a hypothetical $2,400 campaign generating 40 inquiries has a $60 cost per inquiry. If only 24 qualify, cost per qualified inquiry is $100. These are not industry benchmarks.</p></section>
<section class='lf-article-section'><h2>Measure cost per confirmed estimate</h2><p>A confirmed service appointment or project estimate is more meaningful than an unverified calendar interaction. Use the scheduling provider or CRM to confirm actual bookings where supported.</p><p>If the same hypothetical $2,400 campaign creates 12 confirmed estimate appointments, cost per booked estimate is $200. This does not establish whether the jobs are profitable.</p></section>
<section class='lf-article-section'><h2>Compare different electrical services</h2><p>Minor electrical repairs, panel replacements, EV charger installations and commercial electrical projects have different revenue opportunities, material expenses, labor requirements and timelines.</p><p>Compare acquisition costs against realistic contribution margins for each service instead of using one target for every electrical inquiry.</p></section>
<section class='lf-article-section'><h2>Include the full cost of customer acquisition</h2><p>Agency services, creative development, landing pages, software and the staff time needed to respond to prospects may affect the total cost of acquiring customers.</p><p>Separate one-time setup costs from recurring expenses and evaluate contribution margin rather than gross project revenue alone.</p></section>
<section class='lf-article-section'><h2>Make scaling decisions from real results</h2><p>Review qualified leads, confirmed appointments, attended estimates, completed jobs and the value created after direct job costs.</p><p>Advertising costs vary by location, service and competition. Use your own conversion and financial data instead of assuming generic published CPL figures will apply.</p></section>
